    <p>In the 1970s, video games were a novel and simple form of entertainment. Early games like Pong consisted of basic blocks and lines on black-and-white monitors. To experience them, players visited public amusement arcades. [16] These loud, neon-lit rooms quickly became the primary social hubs for teenagers of that generation.</p>
