    text_preview: "Five sentences are missing from this article about the magic of cinema and practical vs digital effects...",
    reading_text: `<h2>Special Effects in Cinema</h2>
    <p>Since the birth of motion pictures, filmmakers have sought ways to bring impossible illusions to life on the silver screen. In the early days of cinema, directors relied entirely on 'practical effects'. These were physical illusions created live on the movie set during filming. [16] Directors used trick camera angles and miniature wooden models to simulate giant monsters or exploding spaceships.</p>
    <p>As the industry developed, mechanical special effects became increasingly sophisticated. In the 1970s and 80s, films like Star Wars and Jaws utilized complex robotic puppets, known as animatronics, to terrify audiences. [17] Actors physically interacted with these machines, which gave their performances a strong sense of realism.</p>
    <p>The true revolution occurred in the 1990s with the introduction of Computer Generated Imagery (CGI). Computers allowed digital artists to create photo-realistic creatures and entire landscapes out of nothing. [18] Jurassic Park was a landmark moment, seamlessly blending practical animatronic dinosaurs with digital versions.</p>
    <p>A recent development in cinematic technology is 'motion capture'. This technique involves an actor wearing a special suit covered in reflective tracking dots while performing in an empty room. [19] This data is then transferred onto a digital model, allowing a human actor's emotions to be seen on an alien creature.</p>
    <p>Today, many directors are choosing to return to practical effects rather than relying solely on computers. They argue that too much CGI makes a movie look like a video game, reducing the dramatic tension. [20] The most successful modern films achieve a perfect balance, using computers only to enhance what was physically recorded by the camera.</p>`,
