    reading_text: `<h2>The Invention of Powered Flight</h2>
    <p>Since ancient times, humans have looked at birds and dreamed of soaring through the skies. Early pioneers constructed artificial wings out of feathers and wood, but their attempts to fly always ended in disastrous failure. In the late 18th century, hot-air balloons allowed humans to lift off the ground. [16] Something more advanced was required to achieve true flight.</p>
    <p>The breakthrough came courtesy of two brothers from Ohio, Wilbur and Orville Wright. Interestingly, neither brother had a university degree or extensive funding. Instead, they ran a small bicycle repair shop, which provided them with mechanical skills and profits to invest in their hobby. [17] They realized that controlling the aircraft once it was in the air was the key obstacle.</p>
    <p>To study aerodynamics, they constructed a miniature wind tunnel inside their bicycle shop, testing more than two hundred different wing designs. They moved their actual flight experiments to Kitty Hawk, a remote beach in North Carolina. [18] The strong, steady ocean breezes there provided the ideal conditions for lifting their gliders into the air.</p>
    <p>On December 17, 1903, the brothers made history. Orville climbed into their motorized wooden aircraft, called the Flyer, and took off from a wooden track. [19] The flight lasted a mere twelve seconds and covered just thirty-six meters, but it was the first controlled, powered flight in human history.</p>
    <p>The invention of the airplane altered the shape of human civilization forever. Within a few decades, aviation developed from a dangerous experimental hobby into a massive global industry. [20] The Wright brothers' bicycle shop experiments had successfully shrunk the physical distance between nations forever.</p>`,
