    title: "The Story of Chocolate",
    text_preview: "Complete the gaps in this reading text about the origins and transformations of chocolate...",
    reading_text: `<h2>The Story of Chocolate</h2>
    <p>Today, chocolate is a universally loved treat, available in countless flavors inside sweet shops around the world. However, the original chocolate consumed by ancient civilizations was completely different from the sweet bars we enjoy today. [16] They harvested beans from the cacao tree and ground them into a thick liquid paste.</p>
    <p>This paste was mixed with water, chili peppers, and cornmeal to create a frothy, spicy beverage. The Aztecs considered this drink sacred, believing it was a gift from the gods that provided wisdom and physical stamina. [17] In fact, cacao beans were so highly prized that they were utilized as a form of cash currency to buy food and clothing.</p>
    <p>Spanish explorers encountered this strange beverage in the 16th century and brought it back to Europe. The bitter taste was initially unpopular among Europeans, who found it unpalatable. [18] This modification transformed the drink into an expensive luxury item consumed exclusively by royal families and wealthy aristocrats.</p>
    <p>The Industrial Revolution in the 19th century transformed chocolate production completely. Engineers invented steam-powered machinery that could press cacao beans quickly, separating the fat from the powder. [19] Soon after, a British company named Fry & Sons created the world's first solid eating chocolate bar by mixing sugar back into the smooth paste.</p>
    <p>In 1875, Swiss inventor Daniel Peter added condensed milk to the recipe, creating milk chocolate, which quickly became the most popular version globally. [20] Today, sustainability is the primary challenge facing the industry, as consumers demand fair wages for cacao farmers in West Africa.</p>`,
