    title: "The Dawn of Space Tourism",
    text_preview: "Insert the correct sentences into this text about regular citizens traveling beyond Earth...",
    reading_text: `<h2>The Dawn of Space Tourism</h2>
    <p>During the mid-20th century, space exploration was an exclusive arena controlled by powerful superpower governments. The 'Space Race' between the United States and the Soviet Union was driven by military technology and national pride. Only highly trained military pilots, known as astronauts, had the opportunity to leave the atmosphere. [16] Private companies are now offering regular citizens the chance to see the Earth from orbit.</p>
    <p>The concept of space tourism officially began in 2001, when American businessman Dennis Tito paid twenty million dollars to ride a Russian rocket to the International Space Station. He spent seven days living alongside professional astronauts. [17] Since then, technological breakthroughs have reduced costs, allowing visionary aerospace corporations to build custom spaceplanes.</p>
    <p>These modern commercial spacecraft do not use traditional vertical rocket structures. Instead, they are carried high into the sky by a massive carrier airplane before igniting their rocket engines to glide into suborbital space. [18] Passengers experience approximately four minutes of complete weightlessness, allowing them to float around the cabin while looking at the curvature of the Earth.</p>
    <p>However, the industry faces severe criticism from environmental scientists. Rocket launches release immense amounts of carbon emissions directly into the upper layers of the atmosphere, where they remain for years. [19] Many argue that spending millions on a four-minute ride is irresponsible when our own planet faces a climate crisis.</p>
    <p>Despite these valid worries, the demand for tickets is soaring among wealthy individuals, with waiting lists extending years into the future. [20] Whether space tourism remains an exclusive playground for billionaires or develops into a regular commercial industry remains to be seen.</p>`,
