    title: "The Rise of E-Books",
    text_preview: "Five sentences have been removed from this text about digital reading technology. Match them up...",
    reading_text: `<h2>The Rise of E-Books</h2>
    <p>For more than five centuries, the physical printed book was the undisputed king of information distribution. Ever since Johannes Gutenberg invented the printing press in Germany, paper pages bound inside leather covers formed the foundation of education and literature. [16] The introduction of electronic ink technology created a digital alternative to paper libraries.</p>
    <p>The true turning point occurred in 2007 with the launch of portable e-readers like the Amazon Kindle. Unlike bright computer monitors that cause severe eye strain, these devices use 'e-ink' displays that mimic real paper. [17] Furthermore, their batteries can last for weeks because they only consume electrical power when a user turns a page.</p>
    <p>The primary advantage of e-books is their incredible convenience. Instead of packing heavy paper volumes into a holiday suitcase, a traveler can store thousands of digital books on a single slim device. [18] This feature has completely transformed how students manage their textbook loads at university campuses globally.</p>
    <p>Initially, many traditional publishers feared that digital devices would cause the total disappearance of physical independent bookstores. They worried that paper books would suffer the same fate as music CDs after the arrival of digital downloads. [19] Many readers discovered that they missed the physical sensation of turning real pages and the smell of paper books.</p>
    <p>Today, the publishing market has reached a surprising and stable balance. Sales statistics reveal that e-books and printed books coexist peacefully, serving different reading needs. [20] It appears that the ancient invention of paper pages is far more resilient than technology experts predicted.</p>`,
