    title: "The Biological Importance of Sleep",
    text_preview: "Read this health article about why our brains require sleep and fill in the missing sentences...",
    reading_text: `<h2>The Biological Importance of Sleep</h2>
    <p>In our modern, fast-paced world, sleep is frequently viewed as a luxury or a waste of productive time. Many students and corporate professionals boast about surviving on just four or five hours of rest a night, using strong coffee to stay alert at their desks. [16] Science proves that cheating your body of rest has severe biological consequences.</p>
    <p>While your muscles relax during sleep, your brain enters a state of intense, highly organized activity. One of its primary tasks is memory consolidation. [17] Without adequate sleep, the brain struggles to organize this data, which explains why sleep-deprived students perform poorly in school exams.</p>
    <p>Furthermore, recent medical studies have revealed that sleep acts as a neurological cleaning system. During deep sleep cycles, fluid flows between brain cells quickly, flushing out toxic waste proteins that build up during daylight hours. [18] If you remain awake all night, these harmful materials accumulate and interfere with normal cognitive functions.</p>
    <p>The physical body also relies on sleep for repair and hormonal balance. While you sleep, your immune system releases specialized proteins called cytokines, which help fight off dangerous infections and reduce bodily inflammation. [19] This explains why you often feel intensely sleepy when you catch a cold or flu virus.</p>
    <p>To optimize your health, doctors recommend maintaining a strict sleep schedule, even during the weekends. Avoiding glowing smartphone screens for an hour before bedtime is another essential tip. [20] Investing in your night rest is the single most effective way to protect your long-term physical and mental wellness.</p>`,
