    title: "Street Art vs. Graffiti",
    text_preview: "Complete this text analyzing the difference between illegal vandalism and public urban art...",
    reading_text: `<h2>Street Art vs. Graffiti</h2>
    <p>Walking through any modern urban city, your eyes will inevitably encounter colorful spray-painted designs on concrete walls, brick buildings, and subway trains. For decades, city councils viewed all public wall painting as a single criminal problem: vandalism. [16] Today, art historians and public galleries draw a sharp distinction between traditional graffiti and street art.</p>
    <p>Traditional graffiti focuses primarily on 'tagging'—writing the artist's secret name or signature using marker pens or simple spray cans. The main goal within the graffiti community is to place your tag in as many difficult, illegal locations as possible. [17] Because speed is essential to avoid being caught by the police, graffiti tags are often done quickly and can look messy to regular citizens.</p>
    <p>In contrast, street art is designed explicitly to communicate with the general public rather than a secret group. Street artists use sophisticated techniques, including massive stencils, painted paper posters, and detailed murals that cover entire apartment blocks. [18] These spectacular visual productions often contain deep messages about social justice or environmental issues.</p>
    <p>The economic impact of street art has forced many city mayors to change their legal approach completely. Instead of spending millions hiring cleaners to erase all paint, cities are now inviting famous artists to paint public walls legally. [19] Neighborhoods that were once avoided by tourists have transformed into vibrant outdoor art museums, packed with trendy cafes.</p>
    <p>Of course, the line between these two formats remains blurry, as seen in the career of the world-famous anonymous artist Banksy. He started his career painting illegal graffiti on train tracks in Bristol. [20] This paradox proves that the relationship between urban art and the law will always remain complex.</p>`,
